(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cp-exam-2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which hypersensitivity type is mediated by IgE fixed on mast cells?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Type I (anaphylactic, immediate) hypersensitivity: IgE made at a first exposure becomes fixed on mast cells and basophils, and re-exposure releases histamine and other substances. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 60, 61"},{"q":"What starts the antigen-dependent phase of B-cell maturation?","choices":["Expression of surface IgM","Release of perforin","Exposure to the antigen","Processing in the thymus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The antigen-dependent phase, in which the B cell differentiates into a plasma cell making specific antibody, is initiated by exposure to the antigen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which layer are smooth muscle cells the predominant element of?","choices":["The intima","The media","The adventitia","The basement membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and their dual role is the point: they are important in normal vascular repair AND play a large part in the development of atherosclerosis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which conditions raise the risk of coagulation in deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Cancer, sepsis, lupus and oral estrogen","Immobility and mechanical compression","Dehydration and thrombocytosis","Trauma and intravenous drug use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the increased coagulability may be genetic or acquired &mdash; these four are the acquired examples given. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What causes most acute pericarditis?","choices":["Most follow myocardial infarction","Most are idiopathic","Most follow cardiac surgery","Most are bacterial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two statements sit together: unexplained in the sense that nothing is identified, but viral in most of those cases. Most of those are viral. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the significance of a large lipid core within a plaque?","choices":["A large core makes the plaque more stable","Plaques with large lipid cores are fragile and rupture","A large core prevents platelet aggregation","A large core indicates the plaque has been cleared"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Size and stability run in opposite directions, which is why the dangerous plaque is not necessarily the one narrowing the lumen most. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What initiates physiologic coagulation?","choices":["Factor XII on contact","External bleeding","Thrombin on fibrinogen","Tissue factor with factor VIIa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Physiologic initiation is tissue factor-VIIa activating factors IX and X; external bleeding is not required. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"Which three main functions does complement serve?","choices":["Antibody secretion, memory, opsonization","Antigen presentation, lysis, memory","Opsonization, inflammation, membrane attack","Neutralization, agglutination, precipitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Complement opsonizes targets for phagocytes, generates inflammatory mediators that recruit and activate immune cells, and forms the membrane attack complex that damages susceptible membranes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 67, 75"},{"q":"Which appearance identifies a band neutrophil on a smear?","choices":["Kidney-bean nucleus indented over half its width","Two-lobed nucleus with large red secondary granules","Round nucleus with scant pale cytoplasm, no granules","Two to three lobes hidden by dark granules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A band neutrophil has a kidney-bean nucleus with mature chromatin and no nucleoli, an indentation greater than half the diameter, and secondary granules only. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 13, 14"},{"q":"What is a primary cause of valvular stenosis?","choices":["Infective endocarditis and papillary muscle rupture","Post-inflammatory scarring from rheumatic fever","Homocysteine-induced vascular injury","Coxsackievirus infection of the valve leaflets"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Post-inflammatory scarring from rheumatic fever is one of the two primary causes of stenosis; the other is valvular calcification with aging. The narrowed opening forces the heart to do extra pressure work. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the characteristic lesion of sarcoidosis?","choices":["Caseating granulomas with necrosis","Hyaline membranes in the alveoli","Subpleural apical blebs","Noncaseating epithelioid granulomas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis develops multiple, uniform, noncaseating epithelioid granulomas, most commonly in the lymph nodes and lung, surrounded by large histiocytes. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What triggers renin secretion?","choices":["A rise in pressure in the afferent arterioles","A rise in plasma sodium concentration","A fall in pressure in the afferent arterioles","Direct sympathetic stimulation of the adrenal medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The kidney senses the pressure drop at the afferent arteriole and responds, which is the first step of the sequence that restores it. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does a low FEV1/FVC (forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity) ratio identify?","choices":["A restrictive pattern","Incomplete emptying","A gas-transfer problem","Expiratory airflow limitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low FEV1/FVC (forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity) ratio identifies expiratory airflow limitation, the obstructive pattern. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How is a relative white cell count converted to an absolute count?","choices":["Divide the cell's percentage by the total white count","Subtract the lymphocyte count from the total white count","Multiply the total white count by the cell's fraction","Read the cell's percentage straight off the differential"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absolute count is the white blood cell count times the relative count expressed as a fraction: a total of 8,000 cells/µL with 25 percent neutrophils gives 2,000 neutrophils/µL. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What happens to left ventricular stroke volume in mitral stenosis?","choices":["It increases to compensate","It is unchanged until the valve closes completely","It becomes dependent on atrial contraction alone","It decreases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ventricle is not diseased; it is simply not being filled, which is why the problem is upstream of it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the consequence of endothelial repair for nitric oxide?","choices":["Its production increases sharply","It is unchanged by injury","It is replaced by carbon monoxide signaling","Its production decreases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and because nitric oxide is a vasodilator and an inhibitor of platelet adhesion, losing it shifts the vessel toward constriction and thrombosis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is bandemia?","choices":["More immature neutrophils in the blood","More mature neutrophils in the blood","Fewer than 200 neutrophils/µL","More eosinophils from a drug effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bandemia is an increase in immature (band) neutrophils, a left shift, which may accompany infection or other inflammatory stress. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which antibodies are usually present in the plasma of a group O person?","choices":["Anti-A without anti-B","Anti-A and anti-B","Anti-B without anti-A","Neither anti-A nor anti-B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Group O red cells carry neither A nor B antigen, and group O plasma usually carries both anti-A and anti-B. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does neutralization protect without destroying the target?","choices":["It clumps the targets into lattices","It blocks an essential host interaction","It coats the targets for phagocytes","It assembles pores in their membranes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibody binding blocks an essential interaction with a host cell, preventing viral attachment or entry or a toxin reaching its target, and limits activity without physical destruction. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which sex and age groups are listed as at risk?","choices":["Premenopausal women specifically","Children and adolescents","Men only, at any age","Men and postmenopausal women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, together with increasing age &mdash; the pairing implies that the premenopausal state is relatively protective. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What keeps lymph moving along the larger lymphatic vessels?","choices":["Muscle movement, breathing and arterial pulsation","Contraction of the heart alone","Gravity draining it down toward the feet","Cilia beating along the lymphatic lumen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Skeletal muscle movement, pressure changes in the thorax during breathing and the pulsations of adjacent arteries help propel lymph, while the vessel's valves keep it moving forward. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which defect underlies X-linked agammaglobulinemia?","choices":["Abnormal thymic development","Impaired B-cell maturation","Profound T-cell dysfunction","Medication-induced loss of function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. X-linked agammaglobulinemia is impaired B-cell maturation with very low antibodies, and is associated with recurrent pyogenic bacterial infections. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which anemia results from diminished erythropoiesis?","choices":["Hereditary spherocytosis","Anemia of acute blood loss","Extravascular hemolytic anemia","Aplastic anemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The anemias of diminished erythropoiesis are megaloblastic anemia, vitamin B12 deficiency anemia, iron deficiency anemia and aplastic anemia: too little production rather than loss or destruction. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why do the lungs in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease fail to deflate normally?","choices":["Air is trapped in damaged air sacs","The pleural space fills with air","Surfactant production rises","The alveolar walls stiffen with fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lungs do not deflate like normal lungs because air is trapped in damaged air sacs, while bronchioles narrow and block with mucus. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 30, 32, 55"},{"q":"What defines dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Cardiac failure with dilation of one or both ventricular chambers","A thickened, hyperkinetic ventricular muscle mass","A stiff, fibrotic ventricle with impaired filling","Inflammation with leukocyte infiltration and necrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is named for the chamber change, and the causes listed for it are otherwise unrelated to one another. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How is atherosclerosis characterized as a process?","choices":["A purely degenerative process of aging","A chronic inflammatory and healing response","An infectious process of the arterial wall","A congenital malformation of the intima"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis is a chronic inflammatory and healing response of the arterial wall to endothelial injury, which then progresses through lipoprotein accumulation, cell recruitment, lipid accumulation and calcification. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What kind of disorder is polycythemia vera?","choices":["An erythropoietic response to hypoxia","A fall in plasma from dehydration","A clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm","A myelodysplastic marrow neoplasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polycythemia vera is a clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm: an absolute erythrocytosis from clonal proliferation of myeloid cells, listed with chronic myeloid leukemia, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 18"},{"q":"What happens to the aortic wall in dissection?","choices":["The intima thickens progressively, narrowing the lumen","The media calcifies without affecting the lumen","The whole circumference dilates uniformly","The laminar planes of the media split apart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and hypertension is the major risk factor, though Marfan syndrome and inflammation can also produce it. A blood-filled channel forms within the wall. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Besides circulating monocytes, what is another origin of resident tissue macrophages?","choices":["Development before birth, then local renewal","Neutrophils settling in inflamed tissue","Lymphoblasts maturing in the thymus","B cells changing after antigen exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes enter tissue during inflammation and become macrophages, but some resident macrophages develop before birth and renew locally. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is a prerequisite for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Pre-existing calcification of the valve","A recent myocardial infarction","Invasion of the bloodstream by the organism","Immune cross-reactivity with streptococcal antigens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The valve is reached through the blood, so bacteremia has to come first. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"In acute leukemia, acquired mutations in which genes block the differentiation of immature cells?","choices":["Immunoglobulin genes","Coagulation factor genes","Red-cell membrane genes","Transcription factor genes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acquired mutations in transcription factors, which regulate the differentiation of immature lymphoid or myeloid cells, produce the maturation arrest of acute leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Early iron deficiency usually falls in which mean cell volume group?","choices":["Microcytic","Normocytic","Macrocytic","Megaloblastic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Established iron deficiency is microcytic, but EARLY iron deficiency is listed with the normocytic anemias (mean cell volume 80 to 100 fL, femtoliters), before the cells have shrunk. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which of the three alveolar patterns of diffuse interstitial lung disease is reversible?","choices":["Inflammation","Fibrosis","Destruction","Bullae formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The immune response shows three patterns: inflammation (early and reversible), fibrosis, and destruction (end stage). — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"On room air, which arterial oxygen tension (PaO2) defines the hypoxemia of acute respiratory failure?","choices":["Below 40 mmHg","Below 50 mmHg","Below 60 mmHg","Below 70 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute respiratory failure is disturbed gas exchange with arterial oxygen below 60 mmHg, carbon dioxide above 50 mmHg or pH below 7.30, breathing room air (21% oxygen). — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Lupus is an example of which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lupus is a type III immune-complex example, alongside immune-complex glomerulonephritis or arthritis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which are given as uncommon causes of coronary heart disease?","choices":["Coronary vasospasm and thrombosis","Reduced oxygen content of the blood, and poor perfusion","Familial hypercholesterolemia and high fat diet","Rheumatic fever and infective endocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both reach the same endpoint from outside the coronary artery: the vessel may be open, but what flows through it either carries too little oxygen or arrives at too low a pressure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What determines the risk that a plaque will rupture?","choices":["Its age alone","Its composition and mechanical stress","The degree of luminal narrowing alone","The lipoprotein that delivered the lipid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rupture risk depends on the composition of the plaque and on the mechanical stresses that act on it. A vulnerable plaque has a large necrotic core covered by a thin fibrous cap. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"When does community-acquired pneumonia develop?","choices":["After 4 days in hospital","When normal defenses are compromised","When surfactant is overproduced","When the pleural space fills with air"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal defenses (immune responses, cough reflex, sneezing, mucociliary clearance) protect the lung; community-acquired pneumonia occurs when they are compromised. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 106, 108, 109"},{"q":"Approximately what share of whole blood is plasma?","choices":["35 percent","45 percent","55 percent","65 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whole blood is approximately 55 percent plasma and 45 percent formed elements, and plasma itself is about 90 to 92 percent water carrying electrolytes, nutrients, wastes and proteins. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does multiple myeloma produce hypercalcemia?","choices":["Renal injury from monoclonal proteins","Marrow infiltration crowding red cells","Altered bone remodeling","Impaired immunity from abnormal antibodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In myeloma, altered bone remodeling can produce osteolytic lesions and hypercalcemia; the monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration separately cause renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which lung carcinoma grows fastest, doubling in about 33 days?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Small cell (oat cell) carcinoma grows rapidly, doubling in about 33 days, with common widespread metastasis and paraneoplastic syndrome. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 25, 26, 27"},{"q":"What determines an immunoglobulin's class?","choices":["The heavy-chain constant region","Whether the light chain is kappa or lambda","The variable-region sequence","The number of disulfide bonds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The heavy-chain constant region determines immunoglobulin class (heavy chain mu, delta, gamma, epsilon or alpha). — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is relative erythrocytosis?","choices":["An increase in red-cell mass","A clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm","A marrow response to hypoxia","Less plasma without more red-cell mass"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Relative erythrocytosis is reduced plasma volume without increased red-cell mass, such as dehydration; hemoglobin and hematocrit rise only because the plasma has shrunk. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What happens to the airways in acute bronchitis?","choices":["Destruction of alveolar walls without fibrosis","Permanent dilation of the bronchi","Inflammation, more mucus, lost ciliary function","Gland hypertrophy over successive years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In acute bronchitis the airways are inflamed, narrow and swollen, with increased mucus, loss of ciliary function and loss of portions of the ciliated epithelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"Which four functions does the vascular system serve?","choices":["Gas exchange and the production of blood cells","Immune surveillance and antibody synthesis","Transport of nutrients, wastes, hormones; homeostasis","Thermoregulation and acid production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nutrients go to tissues, wastes come away from them, hormones travel from one part of the body to another, and fluid balance is maintained across all tissues so cells can function. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does a nonthrombogenic endothelial surface achieve?","choices":["It accelerates clot formation at rest","It prevents leukocytes from entering tissues anywhere","It stops all molecules crossing the vessel wall","It keeps blood in a fluid state"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is an active property rather than a passive one, which is why endothelial injury is the event that lets platelets adhere and a thrombus begin. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the immediate metabolic consequence of reduced oxygen supply to cardiac cells?","choices":["The formation of adenosine triphosphate falls","Lactate is converted back into glucose within the cell","Calcium is actively pumped out of the cell more rapidly","Oxygen extraction from the blood falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cell loses its energy currency first, and everything that follows -- failure to contract, then death -- is downstream of that. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which condition is associated with an EXUDATIVE pleural effusion?","choices":["Severe heart failure","Nephrotic syndrome","Pulmonary embolism","Cirrhosis with ascites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exudates are associated with malignancies, infections, pulmonary embolism, sarcoidosis, post-myocardial infarction syndrome and pancreatic disease. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"What defines hypersensitivity?","choices":["Immune reactions that injure the body","Any immune response to a foreign antigen","Failure to produce enough antibody","Infection with a pyogenic organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity is when immune reactions, either humoral or cellular, result in injury to the body; four types are seen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is vasculitis?","choices":["Vessel wall thickening with loss of elasticity","Dilation of a vessel from wall weakening","Thrombus formation within a deep vein","Wall inflammation, mostly small vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct &mdash; arterioles, capillaries and venules, although any organ and any vessel can be affected. Manifestations follow from the location of the vessel involved. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does capillary diameter compare with a red blood cell?","choices":["About ten times larger","About a hundred times larger","Too variable to compare","Equal to or even slightly smaller"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A capillary is equal to or even slightly smaller than a red blood cell, and it has no media: just an endothelial lining for exchange. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What ruptures in a primary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["A bronchus from penetrating trauma","A cyst from preexisting lung disease","The thoracic duct","Small subpleural blebs at the apices"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary pneumothorax results from rupture of small subpleural blebs in the apices: air enters the pleural space, the lung collapses and the rib cage springs out. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"What thickens the walls of small pulmonary vessels in pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Lipid plaques in the intima","Thinning of the media","More muscle mass in the media","Calcium in the adventitia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic exposure thickens the small pulmonary vessel walls through increased muscle mass of the media, driven by local tissue hypoxia and acidosis, and the intima becomes fibrotic. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which effusion type follows heart failure or hypoproteinemia?","choices":["A chylous collection","A serous transudate","A hemopericardium","A serosanguinous effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both causes act by altering the forces governing fluid movement rather than by injuring anything. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"On what does the result of vessel occlusion depend?","choices":["Collateral circulation","The lipoprotein pattern of the patient","Which valve is nearest the occluded segment","The presence of pericardial fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The same occlusion in two hearts produces different damage, which is why the vessel alone does not predict the outcome. Workload and the length of time. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which form immediately precedes the segmented neutrophil?","choices":["Band form","Metamyelocyte","Myelocyte","Monoblast"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil line runs promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, then the band form, then the segmented neutrophil. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which finding fits non-allergic (intrinsic) asthma?","choices":["An IgE-mediated immediate phase","A more favorable prognosis","A specific allergen identified","Negative skin tests, normal serum IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intrinsic asthma has no identifiable specific allergen, negative skin tests to common inhalant allergens and normal serum IgE; it often develops in middle age. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Hemophilia A is a deficiency of which factor?","choices":["Factor IX","Factor V","Factor VIII","Factor VII"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemophilia A is factor VIII deficiency; hemophilia B is factor IX deficiency. Both factors work together, IXa with its cofactor VIIIa activating factor X. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are the early valve changes of rheumatic endocardial inflammation?","choices":["Immediate calcification of the leaflets","Valvular swelling and erosions","Ballooning of the leaflets into the atrium","Rupture of the papillary muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endocardial inflammation causes valvular swelling and erosions, with platelet aggregation and fibrin on the leaflets. Scarring and shortening of the valvular structures appear only with progression, which is why rheumatic fever is a cause of stenosis. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which three are named as weakening the wall through smooth muscle loss or inappropriate matrix synthesis?","choices":["Ischemia, hypertension and tertiary syphilis","Marfan syndrome, lupus and rheumatoid arthritis","Smoking, diabetes and hyperlipidemia","Giant cell arteritis, polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they form one of three routes to wall failure, alongside poor connective tissue quality and an imbalance of collagen degradation against synthesis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
